          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01YL7a3SzAUftiSU4ijiybVe",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022pages/concepts/credential-and-path-composition.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Credential and Path Composition\\ndescription: How credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions both centre on single, validated composition points.\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Credential-and-Path-Composition\\n  - tech/dotnet\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both pages explicitly describe centralized composition strategies applied to different concerns (secrets vs. paths); the parallel pattern is implicit but consistent across both decisions.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2027-02-14\\n---\\n\\n[[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] both solve their respective problems \u2014 keeping credentials safe and ensuring every file location is known and validated \u2014 using the same architectural pattern: **a single, centralized composition point that runs once at startup and distributes resolved values to child processes or downstream code.**\\n\\n**Credential Scoping** accomplishes this by having the Hub\u0027s secrets loader read the Anthropic API key exactly once at startup and inject it directly into the child process\u0027s environment, without ever touching the Hub\u0027s own environment. The credential flows through a single, controlled channel.\\n\\n**Runtime Path Configuration** achieves an analogous goal by having a single path resolver read all runtime locations (wiki root, data directory, database, secrets file, agent instruction directories) exactly once at startup, validate that every required location exists, and then provide the entire resolved set to downstream code \u2014 preventing any part of the codebase from rediscovering paths on its own.\\n\\nThe parallel is deeper than mere organizational similarity: both decisions stem from the principle that sensitive or environment-dependent state should be resolved once, at a known entry point, with full visibility and validation, rather than scattered across multiple components. For credentials, the payoff is containment of a breach surface; for paths, the payoff is correctness and maintainability when adding new locations.\\n\u0022}"
