      "assistant_text": "## Answer\n\nBoth decisions follow the same architectural pattern: **centralized, validated composition at startup.**\n\n[[credential-scoping]] solves credential safety by having the Hub\u0027s secrets loader read the API key exactly once at startup and inject it directly into the child process\u0027s environment\u2014never letting it touch the Hub\u0027s own process. The credential flows through a single, controlled channel.\n\n[[runtime-paths]] solves runtime location correctness by having a single path resolver read all file locations (wiki root, data directory, database, secrets file, agent instruction directories) exactly once at startup, validate they exist, and distribute the resolved set to downstream code. No component rediscovers paths on its own.\n\nThe connection is that both reject the scatter-and-discover model. Rather than having each component find credentials or paths as needed, both decisions concentrate state resolution at one entry point with full visibility and validation. For credentials, that containment limits breach surface; for paths, it ensures maintainability when adding new locations.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/credential-and-path-composition]], since it bridges two design decisions and reveals a shared principle neither page explicitly states on its own.",
